Use 28-bit libtommath digits on 64-bit Windows

The bundled libtommath switches to 60-bit digits whenever __x86_64__ is
defined, and declares mp_digit as unsigned long for them. That takes a 64-bit
long. On 64-bit Windows long has 32 bits, so under MinGW GCC and Clang every
digit loses its upper bits, as GCC points out dozens of times:

    warning: left shift count >= width of type [-Wshift-count-overflow]
    warning: conversion from '__int128 unsigned' to 'mp_digit' {aka 'long
    unsigned int'} changes value from '384307168202282325' to '1431655765'

The RSA code built on it breaks down with no error reported to the caller.
SFileSignArchive leaves an all-zero weak signature in the archive, which
SFileVerifyArchive then accepts as "zeroed out", and an archive that carries a
real weak signature fails with ERROR_VERIFY_FAILED.

MSVC never defines __x86_64__, so upstream's own 64-bit Windows builds use the
default 28-bit digits, as every 32-bit build does. Do the same for any _WIN64
build.

Upstream: not submitted yet
Applies-to: ucrt64, clang64 (no effect on mingw32)

diff --git a/src/libtommath/tommath.h b/src/libtommath/tommath.h
index 1ead3d0..680f1cb 100644
--- a/src/libtommath/tommath.h
+++ b/src/libtommath/tommath.h
@@ -45,8 +45,9 @@ extern "C" {
 #endif
 
 
-/* detect 64-bit mode if possible */
-#if defined(__x86_64__) 
+/* detect 64-bit mode if possible -- but not on 64-bit Windows, where long,
+ * and with it mp_digit below, is only 32 bits wide */
+#if defined(__x86_64__) && !defined(_WIN64)
    #if !(defined(MP_64BIT) && defined(MP_16BIT) && defined(MP_8BIT))
       #define MP_64BIT
    #endif
